<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vidin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vidin is a port town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, built on the site of Roman Bononia. A stronghold of the medieval Bulgarian states, it was from 1356 the capital of the Tsardom of Vidin under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-sratsimir">Ivan Sratsimir</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1396. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baba-vida">Baba Vida fortress</a> is one of the best-preserved medieval castles in Bulgaria, and around 1800 it was the base of the rebel pasha <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-pazvantoglu">Osman Pazvantoğlu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vidin.jpg" alt="Vidin" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Erik Cleves Kristensen · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baba_Vida_Castle_(27379217451).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
